d $b$ are real numbers, $a^2b^3=\\frac{32}{27}$, and $\\frac{a}{b^3}=\\frac{27}{4}$, what is $a+b$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6070de8b08a519264e7d485488ce166b62b678acb66016ce444d375e82c0617d"},"seconds":20.761468648910522,"seed":1113322538035828355,"stop":"eos","text":"Now, substitute \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\) back into the equation \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, if \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\), then \\(a = \\frac{2}{9}\\).\n\nNow, calculate \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2}{9} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, find a common denominator:\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{9 \\cdot 3} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{6}{27} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{14}{27}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\nb^6 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\) back into the equation \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, if \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\), then \\(a = \\frac{2}{9}\\).\n\nNow, calculate \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2}{9} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, find a common denominator:\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{9 \\cdot 3} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{6}{27} + \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{14}{27}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}\n\\]","id":"algebra:912"}
